t the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $(-3,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x+3$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3)$ and $(3,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-3]⋃[3,[\\infty ).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-281723/field-1" "q-281723/field-2" "q-281723/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bea8139-82a8-5c0a-9d3b-f3a47356773e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-281723"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-116614/selection/ba6b40bef20acdf29546763a32ab264529c6f6e447c18f5a4ef65fea09db3bdb" :answer/id #uuid "d412c8f5-eee9-5ccf-b383-fd78e60c2c61" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-7]\\cup {0}\\cup [4,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116614/selection/960aa5ffb738b7a38f692fa6866aeedd0e1e1360bda60b617ba78dcf40bcf1c4" :answer/id #uuid "ae141b53-8e11-5fd1-8a03-6c5588f83784" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in [-7,4]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116614/selection/f858307447356dfc3f2c87ad67d174d6ed961fb9f4181b982f01a6db513e0b37" :answer/id #uuid "d7a6f50b-47b0-5012-8f98-2cad9a252dd1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-7]\\cup [4,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116614/selection/9ebf1a37a33f1d81100e000e3bb63551a1a45be650ee5d79e2858120ac45347c" :answer/id #uuid "9de9e2d2-5390-5211-b0ff-9a675f812d24" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-7]\\cup {0}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116614/selection/6991c43188ddf95e08466b02cc9be86c2c00051ab8b4152673df11ab576d6695" :answer/id #uuid "e98a5678-6111-596a-93a8-62fe1465286a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in {0}\\cup [4,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116614/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "738ed013-6361-5810-819c-4fd1391228f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-116614/selection/ba6b40bef20acdf29546763a32ab264529c6f6e447c18f5a4ef65fea09db3bdb" :answer-field/choices ["q-116614/selection/ba6b40bef20acdf29546763a32ab264529c6f6e447c18f5a4ef65fea09db3bdb" "q-116614/selection/960aa5ffb738b7a38f692fa6866aeedd0e1e1360bda60b617ba78dcf40bcf1c4" "q-116614/selection/f858307447356dfc3f2c87ad67d174d6ed961fb9f4181b982f01a6db513e0b37" "q-116614/selection/9ebf1a37a33f1d81100e000e3bb63551a1a45be650ee5d79e2858120ac45347c" "q-116614/selection/6991c43188ddf95e08466b02cc9be86c2c00051ab8b4152673df11ab576d6695"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-116614" :question/id #uuid "f53c4802-517b-5a9e-91a4-0f3a31ddb623" :question/math-academy-id "q-116614" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${x}^{4}+3{x}^{3}-28{x}^{2}\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the polynomial on the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}+3{x}^{3}-28{x}^{2} & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}+3x-28) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}+7x-4x-28) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x(x+7)-4(x+7)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x-4)(x+7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}(x-4)(x+7).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-7,$ $x=0,$ and $x=4.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals: \n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-7),\\,(-7,0),\\,(0,4),\\,(4,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-7)$ | $(-7,0)$ | $(0,4)$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+7$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| ${x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-4$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-7),$ and $(4,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-7]\\cup {0}\\cup [4,\\infty ).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-116614/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bea8139-82a8-5c0a-9d3b-f3a47356773e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-116614"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-40367"] :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${x}^{4}-1<0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the polynomial on the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-1 & =({x}^{2}-1)({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =(x+1)(x-1)({x}^{2}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+1)(x-1)({x}^{2}+1).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)<0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ These roots divide the number line into $3$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-1),\\,(-1,1),\\,(1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $(-1,1)$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+1$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| ${x}^{2}+1$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)<0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the interval $(-1,1)$ only.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (-1,1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-116695/selection/d383fff844c3f911f75e01a581769993776e37f61c2fc20fcd8f058f2d5f4f68" :answer/id #uuid "dc39290d-bf47-51a0-9f19-0bdf901592bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [7,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116695/selection/b090a176a4969876e0f88fcfc81489134ded69c6d36feaffe6d68c9c40269086" :answer/id #uuid "c5f34431-d56c-5e45-8f74-d08b9489ff27" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in [0,7]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116695/selection/0af9381751df2ed91ba47adbcecb1a69ab1c166100955ef3b541aea51a93361b" :answer/id #uuid "9a649fe3-d5a5-538b-9486-6776577a2765" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0]\\cup [2,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116695/selection/a3d24bbc7c6f2fec77392d4e6a6a1a7799561131f3d501349b1e8dfda6ba4d60" :answer/id #uuid "7af31f6c-3774-5ae8-9124-e4bafdccefc8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in [2,7]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116695/selection/5ddf63d4f5aa149baa6e7425a659192392f793e0afc367ae39982d3eebebe44a" :answer/id #uuid "ea865365-ccd4-5025-8ca7-4c6d79e95681" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup {0}\\cup [7,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116695/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a18dca9c-5e3a-52ba-85be-4cb5824556a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-116695/selection/5ddf63d4f5aa149baa6e7425a659192392f793e0afc367ae39982d3eebebe44a" :answer-field/choices ["q-116695/selection/d383fff844c3f911f75e01a581769993776e37f61c2fc20fcd8f058f2d5f4f68" "q-116695/selection/b090a176a4969876e0f88fcfc81489134ded69c6d36feaffe6d68c9c40269086" "q-116695/selection/0af9381751df2ed91ba47adbcecb1a69ab1c166100955ef3b541aea51a93361b" "q-116695/selection/a3d24bbc7c6f2fec77392d4e6a6a1a7799561131f3d501349b1e8dfda6ba4d60" "q-116695/selection/5ddf63d4f5aa149baa6e7425a659192392f793e0afc367ae39982d3eebebe44a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-116695" :question/id #uuid "1c4e102d-7322-54fc-92ee-3b46bc062dff" :question/math-academy-id "q-116695" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${x}^{4}-7{x}^{2}\\ge 7{x}^{2}+5{x}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Moving all the terms to the left-hand side and factoring the resulting polynomial, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-7{x}^{2} & \\ge 7{x}^{2}+5{x}^{3} \\\\ {x}^{4}-7{x}^{2}-7{x}^{2}-5{x}^{3} & \\ge 0 \\\\ {x}^{4}-5{x}^{3}-14{x}^{2} & \\ge 0 \\\\ {x}^{2}({x}^{2}-5x-14) & \\ge 0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x+2)(x-7) & \\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}(x+2)(x-7).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-2,$ $x=0$ and $x=7.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-2),\\,(-2,0),\\,(0,7),\\,(7,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,7)$ | $(7,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-7$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the interval $(-\\infty ,-2)$ and $(7,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all  $x\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup {0}\\cup [7,\\infty ).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-116695/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c880562-074d-593c-b7dc-80bb39499fb1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-116695"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-282526/field-1/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "ce0abced-e269-5473-b774-8c098cc1c5be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-282526/field-1/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "348ec929-8074-5685-b0a2-3bbb013ad125" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-282526/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "76c978f6-1ad8-5832-8f0e-9f3817990525" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-282526/field-1/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer-field/choices ["q-282526/field-1/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-282526/field-1/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-282526/field-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "8696f310-4f07-5d21-b610-8a8f8b7a39c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-282526/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "24704183-63e6-5b1b-b1f5-0115ecbb2bcf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-282526/field-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-282526/field-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-282526/field-3/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "1da9a5bb-e5a6-511e-a4cd-dbd69d70a43f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-282526/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "fae7f98f-808f-51de-b6e2-d2800a5c4bad" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-282526/field-3/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-282526/field-3/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-282526/field-4/b67476ffce451bf9cc94e82f22f7f4f4b23075d2e02b9ea63c2ad56fd611692c" :answer/id #uuid "9b6310fb-59c4-5474-a8c4-65b3cc26c3e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\infty" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-282526/field-4" :answer-field/id #uuid "88502cf6-e3f8-59eb-a7d2-73a87402230e" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-282526/field-4/b67476ffce451bf9cc94e82f22f7f4f4b23075d2e02b9ea63c2ad56fd611692c" :answer-field/choices ["q-282526/field-4/b67476ffce451bf9cc94e82f22f7f4f4b23075d2e02b9ea63c2ad56fd611692c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-282526" :question/id #uuid "b74a5db9-3027-5ac1-a642-488b8ace7eb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-282526" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${x}^{6}-3{x}^{4}\\ge {x}^{5}+3{x}^{4}.$\n\n$x\\in (-\\infty ,$\n{{field-1}}$]⋃\\,{$\n{{field-2}}$}⋃\\,[$\n{{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "Moving all the terms to the left-hand side and factoring the resulting polynomial, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{6}-3{x}^{4} & \\ge {x}^{5}+3{x}^{4} \\\\ {x}^{6}-3{x}^{4}-{x}^{5}-3{x}^{4} & \\ge 0 \\\\ {x}^{6}-{x}^{5}-6{x}^{4} & \\ge 0 \\\\ {x}^{4}({x}^{2}-x-6) & \\ge 0 \\\\ {x}^{4}(x+2)(x-3) & \\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{4}(x+2)(x-3).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-2,$ $x=0$ and $x=3.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-2),\\,(-2,0),\\,(0,3),\\,(3,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{4}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-2)$ and $(3,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all  $x\\in (-\\infty ,-2]⋃{0}⋃[3,\\infty ).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-282526/field-1" "q-282526/field-2" "q-282526/field-3" "q-282526/field-4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c880562-074d-593c-b7dc-80bb39499fb1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-282526"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-116617/selection/f4b2153dd620072cad8c8a9b97a5a1e00b91e9b0d4a083e19156b3681d9eb1c9" :answer/id #uuid "63e49d70-8d98-53f2-b360-b521bb8180dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116617/selection/a85e034057571184d54ba3f932a98e1b775bb99db40b17cc9d92987ee9edfe0d" :answer/id #uuid "2bf4a545-f3c3-57f0-8e89-805e05ea1591" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-4,4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116617/selection/e85e6ade45bd11baef79da97060af393e9e09d4b0030a82d64346424fd9b7c34" :answer/id #uuid "3adea6e2-39b9-58e9-ae47-4789ddd3a5b3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-4,1)\\cup (1,4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116617/selection/6c7b37f5a5f779ea9a873acbbd61c99927d8e323ee06f2f4155512efffebeaaf" :answer/id #uuid "a8ec0a51-93c2-5baa-bcd1-d39725d9fc01" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-4)\\cup (1,4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116617/selection/12fc2f12fdf3f91b773379e2adceccb409c1f7dbd610d7306fbdc20d08f47528" :answer/id #uuid "a52b32ff-1ef7-5a38-bb83-77624d9f3979" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\in (1,4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116617/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5322fcbf-0af7-50ec-bef5-11da687b4cf3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-116617/selection/6c7b37f5a5f779ea9a873acbbd61c99927d8e323ee06f2f4155512efffebeaaf" :answer-field/choices ["q-116617/selection/f4b2153dd620072cad8c8a9b97a5a1e00b91e9b0d4a083e19156b3681d9eb1c9" "q-116617/selection/a85e034057571184d54ba3f932a98e1b775bb99db40b17cc9d92987ee9edfe0d" "q-116617/selection/e85e6ade45bd11baef79da97060af393e9e09d4b0030a82d64346424fd9b7c34" "q-116617/selection/6c7b37f5a5f779ea9a873acbbd61c99927d8e323ee06f2f4155512efffebeaaf" "q-116617/selection/12fc2f12fdf3f91b773379e2adceccb409c1f7dbd610d7306fbdc20d08f47528"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-116617" :question/id #uuid "f6943d66-bd59-5d44-a697-d43871adf667" :question/math-academy-id "q-116617" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${x}^{2}-{x}^{3}>16-16x.$" :question/worked-solution "Moving all the terms to the left-hand side and factoring the resulting polynomial, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{x}^{3} & >16-16x \\\\ ({x}^{2}-{x}^{3})-(16-16x) & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(1-x)-16(1-x) & >0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-16)(1-x) & >0 \\\\ (x+4)(x-4)(1-x) & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+4)(x-4)(1-x).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)>0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-4,$ $x=1$ and $x=4.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-4),\\,(-4,1),\\,(1,4),\\,(4,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-4)$ | $(-4,1)$ | $(1,4)$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+4$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-4$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $1-x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-4)$ and $(1,4).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-4)\\cup (1,4).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-116617/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c880562-074d-593c-b7dc-80bb39499fb1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-116617"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-282519/field-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "f51d12d6-dc6e-5b19-a8a6-a9cc18be1f49" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-282519/field-1/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "b780761f-01a2-5ee9-8947-c276302e7710" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-282519/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "305d26f4-c969-570c-aea2-11c8c1776e44" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-282519/field-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer-field/choices ["q-282519/field-1/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" "q-282519/field-1/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-282519/field-2/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "435208bc-c164-504e-b364-8d8a2e0b6f55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-282519/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "7bd1ec00-1bf0-5158-9354-69fa65eeff0f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-282519/field-2/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-282519/field-2/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-282519" :question/id #uuid "3b74ab5b-c68c-5ade-b5c2-ac37db3f9080" :question/math-academy-id "q-282519" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${x}^{3}+16x+64\\le 2{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}.$\n\n$x\\in {$\n{{field-1}}$}⋃\\,[$\n{{field-2}}$,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "Moving all the terms to the left-hand side and factoring the resulting polynomial, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+16x+64 & \\le 2{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{3}+16x+64-2{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2} & \\le 0 \\\\ -{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}+16x+64 & \\le 0 \\\\ (-{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2})+(16x+64) & \\le 0 \\\\ -{x}^{2}(x+4)+16(x+4) & \\le 0 \\\\ (-{x}^{2}+16)(x+4) & \\le 0 \\\\ (16-{x}^{2})(x+4) & \\le 0 \\\\ (4-x)(4+x)(x+4) & \\le 0 \\\\ (4-x)(4+x)^{2} & \\le 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=(4-x)(4+x)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\le 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-4$ and $x=4.$ These roots divide the number line into 3 subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-4),\\,(-4,4),\\,(4,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-4)$ | $(-4,4)$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4-x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $(4+x)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\le 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative only on the interval $(4,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\le 0$ for all $x\\in {-4}⋃[4,\\infty ).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-282519/field-1" "q-282519/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c880562-074d-593c-b7dc-80bb39499fb1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-282519"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-271014/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "83970429-32b0-54f2-9534-32f6390fd0ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-271014/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "42952ef4-7093-5cde-ba0b-bfc334794ffe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-271014/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-271014/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-271014/field-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "23dbdbe9-e80c-519c-bbf1-fc073df6ce3b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-271014/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "1124a1b7-13f0-5f69-a047-8e102ebf8e8e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-271014/field-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-271014/field-2/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-271014/field-3/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "1848fc29-e481-54fe-b2e7-bfb9adb1f848" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-271014/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "28d61eba-a6c8-5ca7-bb47-11a2ace513f5" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-271014/field-3/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-271014/field-3/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-271014" :question/id #uuid "3b1653e7-8a6b-537d-835a-1b88dad20709" :question/math-academy-id "q-271014" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${x}^{4}-4x<9{x}^{2}-4x.$\n\n$x\\in (-3,$\n{{field-1}}$)⋃\\,($\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "Moving all the terms to the left-hand side and factoring the resulting polynomial, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-4x & <9{x}^{2}-4x \\\\ {x}^{4}-4x-9{x}^{2}+4x & <0 \\\\ {x}^{4}-9{x}^{2} & <0 \\\\ {x}^{2}({x}^{2}-9) & <0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x+3)(x-3) & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}(x+3)(x-3).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)<0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-3,$ $x=0$ and $x=3.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-3),\\,(-3,0),\\,(0,3),\\,(3,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $(-3,0)$ | $(0,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+3$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)<0,$ we look at the bottom row determine out where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-3,0)$ and $(0,3).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (-3,0)\\cup (0,3).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-271014/field-1" "q-271014/field-2" "q-271014/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c880562-074d-593c-b7dc-80bb39499fb1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-271014"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5827"] :question/problem "Solve the inequality $(2x+1)(x-2)(x-7) > 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x) = (2x+1)(x-2)(x-7).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x) > 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x) = 0$ are $x=-\\dfrac{1}{2},$ $x=2,$ and $x=7.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals: \n\n$$\n\\left( -\\infty,-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right), \\quad \\left( -\\dfrac{1}{2},2 \\right), \\quad (2,7), \\quad (7, \\infty)\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $\\left(-\\infty,-\\dfrac12\\right)$ | $\\left(-\\dfrac12,2\\right)$ | $(2,7)$ | $(7,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2x+1$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-7$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(x)$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)> 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $\\left(-\\dfrac12, 2\\right)$ and $(7,\\infty).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x) > 0$ for all $x \\in \\left(-\\dfrac12, 2\\right) \\cup (7,\\infty).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-7755"] :question/problem "Solve the inequality $x(x+2)^2(x-1) \\leq 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x) = x(x+2)^2(x-1).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x) \\leq 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x) = 0$ are $x=-2,$ $x=0,$ and $x=1.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty,-2), \\quad (-2,0), \\quad (0,1), \\quad (1,\\infty)\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,1)$ | $(1,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $(x+2)^2$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-1$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(x)$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x) \\leq 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the interval $(0,1)$ only.\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\leq 0$ for all $x \\in \\{-2\\} \\cup[0, 1].$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5828"] :question/problem "Solve the inequality $x^4 + 2x^3 - 3x^2 \\leq  0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the polynomial on the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2} & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}+2x-3) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}+3x-x-3) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x(x+3)-(x+3)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x+3)(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^2(x+3)(x-1).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x) \\leq 0.$\n\nThe roots of the polynomial are $x=-3,$ $x=0$ and $x=1.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n( -\\infty,-3 ), \\quad ( -3,0 ), \\quad (0,1), \\quad (1,\\infty)\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-3)$ | $(-3,0)$ | $(0,1)$ | $(1,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x^2$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x+3$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-1$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(x)$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\leq  0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-3, 0)$ and $(0,1).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that the solution to $f(x) \\leq  0$ is\n\n$$\nx \\in [-3,0]\\cup [0,1] =  [-3,1].\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5835"] :question/problem "Solve the inequality $x(2x^2-3) < 5x.$" :question/worked-solution "Moving all the terms to the left-hand side and factoring the resulting polynomial, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(2{x}^{2}-3) & <5x \\\\ x(2{x}^{2}-3)-5x & <0 \\\\ x(2{x}^{2}-3-5) & <0 \\\\ x(2{x}^{2}-8) & <0 \\\\ 2x({x}^{2}-4) & <0 \\\\ 2x(x+2)(x-2) & <0 \\\\ x(x+2)(x-2) & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x) = x(x+2)(x-2).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x) < 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x) = 0$ are $x=-2,$ $x = 0,$ and $x = 2.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty,-2), \\quad  (-2,0),\\quad (0,2),\\quad (2,\\infty)\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $x-2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(x)$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x) < 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\infty, -2)$ and $(0,2).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x) < 0$ for all $x \\in (-\\infty, -2) \\cup (0,2).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
